+ Some work recurses deeply: compiling or evaluating a user's code is the usual case. The stack
+ of a thread is fixed when the thread is made, so such work needs a thread made with a larger
+ one. `threads.stackThreads` is a thread model which gives every task a platform thread of its
+ own with a stack of the size asked for. It takes that size, so it is bound and not imported:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ def depth(count: Int): Int = if count == 0 then 0 else 1 + depth(count - 1)
+
+ val deep =
+   given Threading = threads.stackThreads(512.mib)
+   supervise(async(depth(400000)).await())
+
+ Out.println(deep.show)
+ ```
+
+ #### A thread of its own
+
+ Some foreign state may be touched from one thread only: a compiler and its context are the
+ usual example. A `Lane` is one thread to which work is handed, and which does that work one
+ block at a time, in the order it was submitted. `submit` gives an ordinary `Task` of the
+ block's result, so it is awaited, cancelled and supervised like any other, whichever task
+ submitted it:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val lane = Lane(t"compiler")
+
+ val names = supervise:
+   val first = lane.submit(() => Thread.currentThread.nn.getName.nn.tt)
+   val second = lane.submit(() => Thread.currentThread.nn.getName.nn.tt)
+   (first.await(), second.await())
+
+ lane.close()
+ Out.println(names(0))
+ Out.println((names(0) == names(1)).inspect)
+ ```
+
+ A lane can be given a stack size too, as `Lane(t"compiler", 512.mib)`. Its thread is a daemon,
+ so an open lane does not keep the program alive, and `close` ends it once the work already
+ submitted is done. A lane needs a real thread, so it is not for Scala.js.
+

